# @@NAME@@

A standalone @@DIMENSION@@ game built with the first-party Ferravex facade and
Rust std. Move from the blue starting pad to the gold beacon. This project owns
its Cargo workspace and does not modify the engine workspace or demo catalog.

## Run and test

Requires Rust 1.88+ and Python 3.11+. Run these commands from this directory:

```sh
cargo run --offline --locked --jobs 2
cargo test --offline --locked --jobs 2
```

The native renderer needs the operating system's window/graphics libraries and
a display (on Linux: X11 and OpenGL). See `docs/PLATFORMS.md` in the engine
checkout for platform prerequisites. Headless rule tests need no display.

- **Enter / gamepad A / Play button:** start, resume or retry after winning.
- **WASD / arrow keys / gamepad left stick:** move; diagonals keep the same speed.
- **Escape / gamepad Start / Pause button:** pause or resume.
- **R:** restart the clock and player at the starting pad.
- **F2 / Controls:** inspect and rebind controls. The shipped game expects a
  keyboard or gamepad for movement; a custom touch movement layout is not included.

The host pauses elapsed simulation while unfocused or editing controls. The
game session freezes in Ready, Paused and Won. Rendering keeps running.
For an automated native smoke run on a display:

```sh
cargo run --offline --locked --jobs 2 -- --autoplay --smoke-frames 180
```

## Browser

The Rust `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target must already be installed. If it is
missing, install it explicitly with `rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`.

```sh
python3 -B scripts/build-web.py
python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory target/web
```

Open <http://127.0.0.1:8000/> in a browser with WebGL2. Add `?autoplay` to
automatically start and move toward the beacon. `--profile dev` selects a faster
debug build. The helper stages only this project's `target/web`: it copies the
checkout's first-party browser modules and extracts the shared Rust shaders.
No npm, wasm-bindgen, registry dependencies, CDN or deployment is involved.
Use HTTP; opening `index.html` directly as a file cannot load Wasm modules.

## Edit the game

- `src/game.rs`: renderer-independent rules, `GameSession`, fixed updates and tests.
- `src/lib.rs`: input sampled once per frame, drawing/interpolation, UI and the
  `ferravex::render::game!` native/browser entry point.
- `src/main.rs`: native launcher. Build the library for the browser ABI.
- `web/index.html`: browser page and accessible loading/error status.
- `Cargo.toml`: one absolute local path dependency on the `ferravex` facade.
- `ferravex.json`: versioned generation manifest and engine path used by the web
  helper. If the engine moves, update both this file and `Cargo.toml`.

## Generation options and manifest

From the engine checkout, create a new project with:

```sh
python3 -B scripts/new-game.py moon-garden ../moon-garden --template 2d --tick-rate 120 --window-title 'Moon Garden'
```

Use `--template 3d` for the 3D view. The destination must not exist, even as an
empty directory or symlink; its parent must already exist. Invalid options fail
before any project files are written. Existing projects are never regenerated.

- `--tick-rate HZ`: finite number from 1 to 1000, including fractional rates
  such as 59.94; defaults to 60. This project's generated rate is @@TICK_RATE@@ Hz.
  `Game::TICK_RATE_HZ` in `src/game.rs` is the simulation setting. It is separate
  from the render frame rate. The catch-up budget is eight ticks per frame;
  `FrameStep::dropped_seconds` reports discarded time if rendering falls behind.
- `--window-title TITLE`: 1–120 printable Unicode characters, with nonwhitespace
  content; defaults to the package name. Sets the native window, in-game header,
  browser page/accessibility title and `web/games.json` display title. Quotes,
  backslashes and HTML characters are escaped for their output contexts. The
  package name, input settings owner, browser game ID and Wasm filename stay
  tied to the package name.

`ferravex.json` is UTF-8 JSON with `schema_version: 1`. Its fields are:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `engine_root` | Absolute engine checkout path used by `scripts/build-web.py`. |
| `package` | Cargo package name, also used to locate the built Wasm module. |
| `template` | `2D` or `3D`, preserving the original helper metadata. |
| `tick_rate` | Resolved simulation frequency in Hz, as a JSON number. |
| `window_title` | Resolved title as a JSON string. |
| `generated_files` | Sorted project-relative POSIX paths, including hidden files, `Cargo.lock` and this manifest. |

The options and file list describe generation, with no timestamp or build-output
entries. They do not track later edits or provide runtime configuration. To
customize an existing game, edit `Game::TICK_RATE_HZ`, `window_conf()` and the
`ui::header` call in `src/lib.rs`, and the titles in `web/index.html` and
`web/games.json` as needed. Editing manifest options alone does not change the
compiled game. Keep the manifest with the project so tools can inspect its origin.

`Cargo.lock` is seeded from the engine's first-party dependency closure during
generation. Commit it with your game. If you intentionally change dependencies
or update the engine's dependency graph, update this project's lockfile offline
and review the diff. The engine checkout's lockfile is never rewritten here.

Add gameplay to fixed updates and leave rendering free of simulation mutations.
Use `Game::restart()` so both render poses reset with the session clock. The
rustdoc example on `Game` demonstrates headless start/pause/restart and timing.
The shipped game has held movement and global menu actions only. When adding
gameplay button actions, consume `PlayerInput::pressed` or `PlayerInput::take`
inside actual fixed ticks and pass those action values to renderer-independent
rules. Zero-tick frames must retain pending gameplay edges, and catch-up ticks
must not repeat a press captured once in the render frame. Global start, pause
and restart actions still run once per render frame.
